BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:simons-foundation-open-interval-turbulence-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Interval: An Artist-Scientist Exploration of Turbule
 nce
DESCRIPTION:Open Interval is a Simons Foundation program that supports trio
 s consisting of an artist\, a scientist\, and a representative from a 501(
 c)(3) arts organization to collaborate around a shared scientific theme. T
 he current cycle centers on the theme of turbulence\, a phenomenon at the 
 edge of chaos and beauty. Awarded trios receive USD 30\,000 to support a s
 ustained period of thought partnership and exploration rather than the pro
 duction of finished works or exhibitions. The program runs from April to D
 ecember 2027 with a touchstone meeting in May 2027 and possible cohort con
 venings. Trio members must be regionally co-located anywhere in the United
  States so they can collaborate in person. The scientist should hold a Ph.
 D. and be conducting basic research\, with priority given to fields such a
 s astrophysics\, neuroscience\, quantum physics\, biology\, or mathematics
 . The application portal opens September 9\, 2026\, and all three trio mem
 bers must submit for the application to be complete.\n\nOrganisation: Simo
 ns Foundation\nLocation: United States (trio must be regionally co-located
 )\nAward: USD 30\,000 per awarded trio\nDuration: April to December 2027 (
 several months of collaboration)\n\nMore: https://www.simonsfoundation.org
 /2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-an-artist-scientist-exploration-of-t
 urbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.simonsfoundation.org/2026/06/01/coming-soon-open-interval-a
 n-artist-scientist-exploration-of-turbulence/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States (trio must be regionally co-located)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:legalization-project-immigrant-content-creators-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Legalization Project: Immigrant Content Creators Prog
 ram 2026-27
DESCRIPTION:The Legalization Project is running a nine-month programme for 
 experienced content creators who want to make engaging\, accessible work a
 bout U.S. immigration law\, policy and the issues affecting immigrant comm
 unities. Rather than commissioning one-off pieces\, it pairs a USD 10\,000
  stipend with actual legal grounding: training in U.S. immigration law and
  policy\, ongoing updates from immigration attorneys and advocates\, and c
 ontinuous feedback on the content you make. This suits creators already wo
 rking in video\, social or short-form documentary who want to report accur
 ately on a fast-moving legal area rather than guess at it. ELIGIBILITY: ex
 perienced content creators who are immigrants. The programme is explicitly
  open to immigrants of ALL statuses. APPLICATION: preliminary applications
  are due 17 August 2026\, through the programme page at legalizationprojec
 t.notion.site/icc-program (also circulated as bit.ly/icc-program). Being a
  preliminary application\, expect a second stage after this date.\n\nOrgan
 isation: The Legalization Project\nLocation: United States focus (content 
 covers U.S. immigration law and policy). Programme delivery format not spe
 cified in the call.\nAward: USD 10\,000 stipend\, plus training in U.S. im
 migration law and policy\, regular updates from immigration attorneys and 
 advocates\, and ongoing feedback and support on your content. No applicati
 on fee.\nDuration: Nine months\n\nMore: https://legalizationproject.notion
 .site/icc-program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
URL:https://legalizationproject.notion.site/icc-program?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States focus (content covers U.S. immigration law and polic
 y). Programme delivery format not specified in the call.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-arts-in-community-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts in Community Grants 2027
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Arts in Community Grants fund artist fees for commu
 nity engagement projects featuring visiting Southern artists. Each funded 
 project must include both a public presentation and an educational or comm
 unity engagement component\, so this is not a straight presenting grant. U
 p to USD 10\,000 is available per project against a dollar-for-dollar cash
  match\, with a minimum artist fee of USD 4\,000 and a minimum request of 
 USD 2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: tax-exempt 501(c)(3) nonprofit arts organizations
 \, official units of local\, county or state government\, and federally re
 cognised tribal communities with nonprofit status\, located in the nine-st
 ate South Arts region (Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\
 , Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee). Applicants
  must have a Unique Entity Identifier (UEI). Applications opened 15 July 2
 026\; applicants are notified by email by 24 November 2026\, and grants ar
 e subject to funding availability. Projects must take place between 1 Janu
 ary 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline Tuesday 1 September 2026 at 11:59p
 m ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: USA\, nine-state South Arts r
 egion: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, 
 North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee.\nAward: Up to USD 10\,000 p
 er project\, covering artist fees. A dollar-for-dollar cash match is requi
 red\, so a USD 10\,000 request needs USD 10\,000 in matching funds. Minimu
 m artist fee is USD 4\,000 and the minimum grant request is USD 2\,000. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Projects must take place between 1 January 20
 27 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportun
 ities/arts-community-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/arts-community-grants?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA\, nine-state South Arts region: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, 
 Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and T
 ennessee.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mit-museum-brain-digital-content-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151909Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: MIT Museum: BRAIN Digital Content Open Call
DESCRIPTION:The MIT Museum seeks proposals for compelling digital content e
 xploring humanity's relationship with the BRAIN\, the museum's thematic fo
 cus from April to September 2027 bringing together art\, science\, technol
 ogy and design on cognition\, neurotechnology\, brain-computer interfaces\
 , mental health and the boundaries of ability. Selected proposals run as a
 mbient content on the two-story digital screen (3840x3024\, 80:63 aspect r
 atio\, 20'3" x 16'2") in the museum's central Exchange amphitheater. The m
 useum wants immersive\, non-linear\, experimental\, non-didactic work\, su
 ch as artful visualizations of data or scientific phenomena\; interactivit
 y (touchscreens\, QR codes) is out of scope and content-critical audio is 
 discouraged. Content must run on the museum's existing hardware (Windows P
 C with RTX 3090\, NEC LED wall with Analog Way 4K system). WHO CAN APPLY: 
 artists\, scientists\, engineers\, designers and other creative practition
 ers\, from MIT or the broader community. SUPPORT: flat honorarium of USD 2
 \,500-5\,000 per selected proposal\; no additional development funding. Ap
 plications reviewed from 2 November\, accepted list finalized January 2027
 . Deadline: 1 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: MIT Museum\nLocation: Cambri
 dge\, Massachusetts\, US (content shown on-site\; remote creation possible
 )\nAward: Flat honorarium of USD 2\,500 to 5\,000 per selected proposal\, 
 at the Museum's discretion\nDuration: BRAIN thematic focus runs April - Se
 ptember 2027\; selection finalized January 2027\n\nMore: https://mitmuseum
 .mit.edu/brain-digital-content-open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://mitmuseum.mit.edu/brain-digital-content-open-call?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, Massachusetts\, US (content shown on-site\; remote cre
 ation possible)
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
